#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""book2podcast - fetch a book from Calibre-Web, split it into chapters, and
generate one Open Notebook podcast episode per chapter.

  book2podcast "Thinking, Fast and Slow"              every chapter, saved to ~/Podcasts
  book2podcast "Thinking, Fast and Slow" --list       just show the chapters
  book2podcast "Thinking, Fast and Slow" --chapters 3-7
  book2podcast --file ./book.pdf

Re-running a command resumes: finished episodes are skipped (and downloaded if
missing from the folder).

Chapter detection: candidate breaks come from the EPUB's table of contents,
spine files and headings; a local LLM then judges each candidate (real chapter,
subsection, part title, front/back matter) and titles it. Books with almost no
structure fall back to asking the LLM where chapters change in sliding windows.

PDFs (used when Calibre-Web has no EPUB) first go through Docling on the GPU,
with Ollama and Kokoro unloaded: its layout model recovers headings and drops
page headers/footers, and the PDF's bookmarks act as the table of contents.
Docling exits before the LLM loads, so they never share the GPU.

Work is cached in ~/.cache/book2podcast/<book>/ - edit chapters.json there to
fix a split by hand; it is reused on the next run unless --resplit is given.
"""

